{
  "id": "c20",
  "title": "Files",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c20-l1",
      "title": "Writing a File",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "In Unix, everything is a file. fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") opens a file for writing and gives you a FILE *. \"w\" starts the file empty, \"a\" appends to the end, \"r\" reads.",
        "fprintf(f, …) works exactly like printf, but into the file. When you're done, fclose(f) makes sure everything is saved.",
        "The lessons run on a little simulated Unix disk: files you write stay there until the program ends."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    FILE *f = fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"w\");",
          "    int c;",
          "    fprintf(f, \"unix %d\\n\", 1969);",
          "    fprintf(f, \"c %d\\n\", 1972);",
          "    fclose(f);",
          "    f = fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"r\");",
          "    while ((c = fgetc(f)) != EOF)",
          "        putchar(c);",
          "    fclose(f);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "unix 1969",
          "c 1972"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
