    {
      "id": "c20-l2",
      "title": "Reading Line by Line",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "fgets(line, size, f) reads one line (with its \\n) into a char array, at most size - 1 characters. At the end of the file it returns NULL, so while (fgets(…) != NULL) reads everything.",
        "If a file doesn't exist, fopen(…, \"r\") returns NULL. Always check, or the next read crashes.",
        "This little disk already has a file todo.txt. fscanf(f, \"%d\", &n) reads numbers from a file, like scanf does from the keyboard."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char line[80];",
          "    int n = 0;",
          "    FILE *f = fopen(\"todo.txt\", \"r\");",
          "    if (f == NULL) {",
          "        printf(\"can't open todo.txt\\n\");",
          "        return 1;",
          "    }",
          "    while (fgets(line, 80, f) != NULL) {",
          "        n++;",
          "        printf(\"%d: %s\", n, line);",
          "    }",
          "    fclose(f);",
          "    printf(\"%d tasks\\n\", n);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "1: edit",
          "2: compile",
          "3: test",
          "3 tasks"
        ]
      },
