    {
      "id": "c20-l3",
      "title": "stdin, stdout and stderr",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Every Unix program starts with three files already open: stdin (the keyboard), stdout (the screen) and stderr (also the screen, but for error messages).",
        "printf(…) is really fprintf(stdout, …), and getchar() is fgetc(stdin). So fgets(line, 80, stdin) reads a line typed by the user.",
        "Why a separate stderr? When the output goes into a file or another program, the error messages still show up on the screen."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char name[40];",
          "    fprintf(stdout, \"who are you? \");",
          "    if (fgets(name, 40, stdin) == NULL) {",
          "        fprintf(stderr, \"no input\\n\");",
          "        return 1;",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"\\nhello, %s\", name);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "who are you? ",
          "hello, dennis"
        ],
        "inputs": [
          "dennis"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c20-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What are the three files every program starts with?",
          "options": [
